-# Register cache (--regcache). Lifted code otherwise reads and writes every
-# guest register through ctx->gpr[], and since local-store accesses go through
-# a uint8_t pointer the compiler must assume each one can change any register:
-# every instruction is a load from and a store to the context. With the cache a
-# function keeps the registers it uses in C locals and the context is brought
-# up to date only where something else can look at it. The translator marks
-# those places and _apply_regcache() fills them in:
-#   EXIT  control leaves the function (return, tail transfer, stop, halt):
-#         store the registers changed since the last sync; the path that does
-#         not leave keeps them pending.
-#   OUT   just before a runtime or lifted call: store them, nothing pending.
-#   IN    just after that call: reload every register the function uses, since
-#         the callee (a lifted function, a stop handler, the interpreter) may
-#         have changed any of them.
-# Locals the compiler can prove dead are dropped by it, so the reload and the
-# entry loads cost only what is live.
-_RC_EXIT, _RC_OUT, _RC_IN = "/*@X*/", "/*@O*/", "/*@I*/"
-_RC_MARK = re.compile(r"/\*@[XOI]\*/")
-_RC_GPR = re.compile(r"ctx->gpr\[(\d+)\]")
-_RC_WRITE = re.compile(r"\br(\d+) = ")
-
